ca that it comes to a head  the queen's policy has been to never  express an opinion or pass judgment on  any of the prime ministers  so this was a big deal and as the  credits at the end of episode 8 show the  palace continues to insist  she never said the stuff michael shea  leaked to the press  in the end however thatcher and the  queen spent almost 11 years together  with roughly 164 meetings together  thatcher was in her own words cruelly  torn from her party  and forced to resign but later the queen  would bestow her with the award of merit  an honorary title first awarded by king  edward vii  in 1902 for those who provided  distinguished service to the state  although charles and anne are the more  prominent of the queen's children in the  series we also get small  cameos from princess edward and andrew  and bonus points if you caught the  absolutely creepy scene in which andrew  talks about loving a movie about an  underage girl on an island full of  perverts this was totally the show's nod  to prince andrew having been a close  friend of jeffrey epstein frequently  visiting his island of underage women  switching gears to really understand the  series one must also  understand its masterful use of  juxtaposition and double meaning or as  the french say  double entrangle i just like saying that  episode 1 sees the juxtaposition between  hunting and assassination as montbaton  is stalked and hunted by the ira  so too does the royal family engage in  the hunt  charles fishes montbaton catches lobster  and shoots the stag  and philip hunts birds episode nine's  title engages in double entendre being  called  avalanche not only is this episode about  an actual avalanche the avalanche is a  metaphor for what's happening in charles  and diana's marriage  episode 10 titled war has even more  meanings we see a war between  thatcher and her party war between  charles and diana  war between thatcher and the queen and  an actual war between britain and its  involvement